Authorizes the creation of the Citrus Small and Medium Producers Support Program (Proap Citros).

The National Congress decrees:

Article 1. The Executive Power is authorized to create the Citrus Small and Medium Producers Support Program (Proap Citros).

Article 2º O. The program's main objectives shall be:

I - Ensuring the competitive insertion of small and medium citrus producers in the market;

II - Encouraging the formation of integration mechanisms in the productive chain, involving the agro-industrial sector;

III - Strengthening the internal market for citrus and derived products, including through official marketing campaigns focused on improving the health of the Brazilian population;

IV - Expanding access for small and medium citrus producers to bank credit mechanisms;

V - Providing financial assistance to producers for the implementation and also for the financing of crops;

VI - Developing alternatives for the disposal and storage of production;

VII - Encouraging initiatives for the verticalization of production through producer association;

VIII - Providing specialized technical assistance, focusing on the producer's income generation;

IX - Improving the educational conditions of small producers and their dependents, as well as establishing and expanding professional training, particularly focused on phytosanitary issues and property management;

X - Supporting research for the generation and transfer of technology, including for the production of new varieties;

XI - Encouraging organic production systems;

XII - Providing assistance for the sustainable use of natural resources."

The most relevant agenda items were approved by majority in historic votes within the International Labour Organization (ILO) system. Among them, the following stand out:

1 – Convention and Recommendation on Domestic Workers, approved by 396 votes in favor, 16 against, and 63 abstentions. Undoubtedly, it was one of the most important and memorable events of the 21st century in ILO history and the international trade union movement in the fight for social, human, equality of opportunities, sustainability of the system, and democratic governance, as over 100 million domestic workers and workers worldwide will be affected, with incalculable benefits for society;

2 – Recommendation to the next Conference in 2012 on establishing Social Security floors, approved by 431 votes in favor, 17 against, and 14 abstentions. During this debate, there was extensive discussion on the Social Security floor. While all agreed on a minimum floor, some representatives from countries with more stable social protection systems expressed concern that governments and employers might use the floor to "level down";

3 – Election of the ILO Administrative Council representatives, where our International Relations Secretary, Nilton Souza (Neco), was elected with 105 votes out of 106 in the electoral college to represent all workers and workers in Brazil, joining other councilors and councilors from the global workers' bench from various countries. Through an unprecedented agreement between the International Trade Union Confederation and the All-China Federation of Trade Unions, a single workers' list was presented and elected, including a representative from China as a titular member on the Administrative Council. This initiative was supported by most international trade unions, and in particular by the Trade Union Confederation, as we believe it is important to strengthen the unity and action of workers to face the crisis and challenges of globalization, Neco assessed;

