The Government also announced the inclusion of 189,000 family farmers in the Food Acquisition Program, which provides low-interest financing. This will raise the number of extremely poor rural producers covered by the program from 66,000 to 255,000 by 2014. In total, between poor and medium-income producers, the number of beneficiaries of the PAA will reach 445,000 by the end of Dilma's term. To support the farmers, there will be a team of 11 technicians for every 1,000 families. The plan also provides for the promotion of R$2,400 per family over 2 years to support food marketing and production. Payments will be made through the Bolsa Família card. Additionally, 253,000 families will receive seeds and inputs, such as fertilizers. There will also be a microcredit program aimed at low-income populations. The Government aims to increase the share of resources available in public banks for loans with consulting on the possibilities of applying the money. The goal is to get people to use credit for sustainable consumption, such as financing their own homes. Extreme poverty line.

The Government has defined the extreme poverty line at 70 reais per capita, based on preliminary data from the 2010 Census, released by the Brazilian Institute of Geography and Statistics – IBGE. According to the institute, 11.4 million declared in the census to have a monthly income of up to 70 reais per capita.

Based on IBGE data, the Government has also identified 4.8 million people living in urban or rural areas who do not have exclusive toilets, access to the general sewage network, water distribution network, artesian well, or a spring on their property.

Adding the number of people with an income below 70 reais and without access to water and sewage network, the Government has defined a target of 16.2 million people to be served by the Brazil Without Poverty program.

According to the Ministry of Social Development and Fight Against Hunger, the program's main objective is the promotion of the social and productive inclusion of the population considered extremely poor.

Ten main points of the Brazil Without Poverty program:

1. Take 16.2 million people out of poverty;
2. Qualify 1.7 million people between 16 and 65 years old;
3. Support the productive organization of scrap material collectors;
4. Increase the Bolsa Família benefit limit from 3 to 5 children;

5. Creation of the Green Bolsa for families promoting environmental conservation. Each quarter, families will receive 300 reais;
6. Rainwater harvesting tanks for planting, to be provided to 60,000 rural families;
7. Water tanks for human consumption for 650,000 families;
8. Additional water supply tanks for 262,000 families;
9. Access to electricity for 257,000 families by 2014;
10. Inclusion of 800,000 families in the Bolsa Família program that meet the requirements.
